# Podcast Index API — get keys at https://api.podcastindex.org/
PODCAST_INDEX_KEY=
PODCAST_INDEX_SECRET=

# Spark rail uses @buildonspark/spark-sdk — no API key required (the SDK
# talks directly to Spark's signing operators). Self-custodial; seed lives
# NIP-44-encrypted on the user's Nostr relays.

# Which brand this deploy wears: 'bmb' (default) or 'buddy'.
#
# ONE repo, TWO Vercel projects. boostmebitch.com builds with this unset;
# boostmebuddy.com sets it to 'buddy'. It selects the whole table in
# lib/brand.ts — display name, origin, PWA manifest, boost sound, the
# boostagram app_name and the Nostr `client` tag — so nothing else needs
# changing per deploy.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ because the browser needs it too (the header wordmark, the boost
# modal, the celebration ping). It names a brand, never a secret. Next.js
# inlines it at BUILD time, so changing it needs a redeploy, not a restart.
# Anything unrecognized falls back to 'bmb' rather than to a half-named page.
NEXT_PUBLIC_BRAND=

# Optional: app identity for Podcast Index User-Agent. Absent falls back to the
# active brand's own string ('boostmebitch/0.1' / 'boostmebuddy/0.1').
#
# LEFT UNSET ON PURPOSE. Set, it OVERRIDES the active brand, so a buddy deploy
# whose environment was copied from the bmb project introduces itself to Podcast
# Index and to every third-party feed, chapter, transcript and LNURL host as
# `boostmebitch` — and every `?? BRAND.userAgent` in the code is then dead. The
# brand table is the better default; set this only to override it deliberately.
#APP_NAME=

# Optional: the site's own Nostr identity (server-only — never NEXT_PUBLIC).
# Lets a signed-OUT user still post their boost note to Nostr, signed by the
# app's key via /api/nostr/site-sign. nsec or 32-byte hex. Absent = feature off
# (the "Share on Nostr" checkbox still shows, the note just isn't posted).
SITE_NOSTR_SK=

# Optional: "Continue with Google" onboarding for users with no Nostr key.
# Public by design — an OAuth web client ID is not a secret; the authorized
# JavaScript origins configured on the client are what protect it. Absent =
# the Google button doesn't render at all.
#
# Google Cloud setup: Web OAuth client with origins for the deployed domain
# and http://localhost:3000, Drive API enabled (a fresh project 403s on the
# first Drive call until you do), and BOTH `openid` and `drive.appdata` on the
# consent screen — openid is what makes the userinfo endpoint return `sub`, so
# omitting it fails AFTER consent rather than during it.
#
# drive.appdata is a NON-sensitive scope, so this needs only brand
# verification — no demo video, no third-party security assessment. The
# "unverified app" screen and the 100-user cap come from the consent screen
# sitting in Testing; publishing to In production is what clears them.
#
# Google never sees the Nostr key: it's generated locally, encrypted under a
# PIN-derived key (Argon2id, 6-8 digit PIN -> NIP-44), and only the ciphertext
# is stored in the user's own Drive appdata folder.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# BoostBox metadata service (https://github.com/ChadFarrow/boostbox)
# Used on the LNURL leg of boosts to ship the boostagram out-of-band via the
# LUD-21 comment field. The default URL points at tardbox.com; the API key
# below is the public reference key — replace it with the key for whatever
# BoostBox instance you're hitting (tardbox.com runs its own).
BOOSTBOX_URL=https://tardbox.com
BOOSTBOX_API_KEY=v4v4me

# Optional: the Android app (Trusted Web Activity) published on Zapstore.
# Together these two are what /.well-known/assetlinks.json serves, which is
# what tells Chrome the app may represent this origin — without it the app
# opens with a browser URL bar. Absent = no Android app is authorized here,
# which is the correct answer for any deploy that isn't the real one (a
# preview deployment must NOT vouch for the production package).
#
# ANDROID_CERT_SHA256 is the SHA-256 fingerprint of the APK signing
# certificate: `keytool -list -v -keystore <file> -alias <alias>`, or bare
# lowercase hex from apksigner — either spelling is accepted. Comma-separate
# to carry two during a key rotation; the build already on people's phones is
# signed by the outgoing key, so both must be live at once. See docs/android.md.
#
# WARNING: these are a CREDENTIAL BOUNDARY, not configuration, and they are
# per-ORIGIN. Chrome hands a verified TWA the same profile as the site, and this
# origin's localStorage holds the NWC spending credential and, for a local
# signer, the nsec. So they must name a package built FOR THIS DOMAIN and signed
# by a key you hold. The trap is the second deploy: cloning a Vercel project
# copies every environment variable, so boostmebuddy.com would silently serve a
# handle_all_urls grant for the bmb package. lib/assetlinks.ts fails closed on
# an absent or malformed value, but a COPIED pair validates fine — so both stay
# commented out here, and each project sets its own or neither.
#ANDROID_PACKAGE_ID=
#ANDROID_CERT_SHA256=

# Optional: the Nostr read index (services/nostr-index), deployed separately on
# Railway. It holds relay subscriptions open, stores what it sees, and answers a
# whole feed — notes, their reply forest, quoted events and author profiles — in
# ONE request instead of four serial relay stages. It also keeps a shared
# Podcast Index cache so favorites hydration is a handful of batched requests
# rather than one per favorited show and track.
#
# ABSENT = the feature is off and the app behaves exactly as it did before:
# every read goes to relays and to /api/by-guid as usual. That is deliberate —
# the index is an accelerator, never a dependency, and a preview deploy with no
# index must still work. Both variables are required together; one alone is off.
#
# NOSTR_INDEX_KEY is the shared secret the service checks on every read. It is
# server-only and must never be NEXT_PUBLIC: the app proxies the index through
# /api/nostr/index precisely so the browser never holds it.
NOSTR_INDEX_URL=
NOSTR_INDEX_KEY=
